cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: >-
  downlinkpick: choosing what to downlink when the contact is shorter than the
  queue, with the cost of the choice reported
version: 0.1.0
date-released: 2026-10-21
license: Apache-2.0
abstract: >-
  Selects which recorded data products to downlink over a contact plan that
  cannot carry all of them, and reports what the selection cost: the marginal
  value of the next unit of contact time, and the identity of everything left
  behind with the reason it was left. Two selectors share one problem
  definition. The operational baseline is bundle value-density ordering with two
  window-packing rules and whole-bundle rollback; the exact route is a
  multiple-knapsack mixed-integer program over windows with no-double-downlink,
  per-window capacity and dependency-precedence rows. The MILP is emitted both
  as a PuLP problem, which also writes the portable LP file, and as SciPy
  coefficient arrays from a single model object, and the two emissions are
  checked against each other term by term; PuLP 4.1.0 ships no solver, so the
  optimum is computed by scipy.optimize.milp (HiGHS) and that is recorded rather
  than elided. Validation is level 3. Window capacity, the integral of an
  achievable-rate profile over a pass, agrees with adaptive Gauss-Kronrod
  quadrature to 8.384938e-09 worst relative error over five profiles and with
  the shipped cases to 4.031808e-15; the first run of that check found a
  6.065e-02 relative error in the closed-form integral at a rate step. The MILP
  optimum matches exhaustive enumeration to 0.000000e+00 worst relative
  difference over 240 small instances; the first run of that check found that
  unscaled capacity rows made HiGHS report a wrong optimum with a success status
  on 12 of 240 instances, worst 4.568e-01 relative, which is why every capacity
  row is now normalised by its window capacity. The greedy optimality gap is
  reported as a distribution over 300 seeded instances per family rather than as
  a mean: on the tight family greedy matches the optimum on 51 of 300, with
  median 3.776537e-02, 95th percentile 1.447293e-01 and maximum 2.491356e-01,
  and dependency density costs roughly twice the mean gap that capacity
  tightness does. Four thousand selections from both selectors violate none of
  the no-double-downlink, expiry, capacity, dependency-closure, precedence or
  value-conservation invariants, and the invariant checker is itself shown to
  detect six deliberate corruptions. Selection stability under multiplicative
  value noise is measured rather than asserted: at a relative noise of 0.05 the
  exact selection keeps a Jaccard similarity of 0.99313 and 0.999747 of its
  value, and at 0.40, 0.95919 and 0.992742, so the optimality is worth having at
  every noise level measured against a mean greedy gap of 4.758763e-02. The
  marginal value of contact time is a finite difference on the integral optimum
  and not a dual variable, and it can be negative: a window's end is also its
  delivery epoch, so extending a pass by twenty seconds was measured lowering
  the achievable value by 12.89 per cent when the extension crossed a product's
  expiry. Two learned components are benchmarked against the baselines they have
  to beat and the results differ. The learned value model loses to a complete,
  fresh declaration by a factor of twenty-four in regret fraction, 0.009582
  against 0.000404, and wins once declarations are stale or absent, 0.005799
  against 0.021350 on a case with forty per cent missing and half the remainder
  stale, and 0.008156 against 0.986665 when nothing is declared at all; this is
  the specification's own prediction and it is published as measured. The
  learned compressibility predictor beats every baseline, delivering 0.962760 of
  the oracle's value against 0.900303 for a per-instrument constant table and
  0.827558 for the memoryless entropy floor, and its ensemble dispersion used as
  a conservative size estimate removes every measured window overflow. Both
  learned components are trained on a seeded synthetic generator whose equations
  are printed in its module docstring; no flight data is used anywhere, and
  every learned result is a statement about that generator. OR-Tools CP-SAT is
  the better solver for this class of problem and this package does not compete
  with it; the contribution is the contact-window and expiry accounting and the
  left-behind report, not the search. Research grade. Not flight-qualified, not
  certified, not approved for operational aerospace use.
authors:
  - name: "OPTIMA Organisation"
keywords:
  - downlink-scheduling
  - satellite-communications
  - data-triage
  - multiple-knapsack
  - mixed-integer-programming
  - contact-window
  - ground-station
  - compressibility
references:
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Martello
        given-names: "S."
      - family-names: Toth
        given-names: "P."
    title: "Knapsack Problems: Algorithms and Computer Implementations"
    publisher:
      name: "Wiley"
    year: 1990
    notes: >-
      Chapter 2 for greedy bounds on the 0/1 knapsack and chapter 6 for the
      multiple-knapsack problem, whose assignment formulation is implemented in
      downlinkpick.exact. Structure only; no numerical value in this repository
      is taken from it.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Dantzig
        given-names: "G. B."
    title: "Discrete-variable extremum problems"
    journal: "Operations Research"
    volume: 5
    issue: 2
    year: 1957
    notes: >-
      The linear-relaxation bound obtained by taking items in descending value
      density, implemented as downlinkpick.greedy.dantzig_bound and measured
      loose by a mean of 1.254391e-01 against the proved optimum.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Shannon
        given-names: "C. E."
    title: "A mathematical theory of communication"
    journal: "Bell System Technical Journal"
    volume: 27
    issue: 3
    year: 1948
    notes: >-
      The source-coding theorem, which gives the memoryless compression bound
      H/B implemented as downlinkpick.compress.entropy_floor_ratio. Measured
      above the realised ratio on 0.997778 of held-out products, because a real
      codec exploits correlation the first-order entropy ignores.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Huangfu
        given-names: "Q."
      - family-names: Hall
        given-names: "J. A. J."
    title: "Parallelizing the dual revised simplex method"
    journal: "Mathematical Programming Computation"
    volume: 10
    issue: 1
    year: 2018
    notes: >-
      The HiGHS solver that scipy.optimize.milp calls, and which computes every
      optimum reported by this package.
  - type: generic
    authors:
      - family-names: Mitchell
        given-names: "S."
      - family-names: O'Sullivan
        given-names: "M."
      - family-names: Dunning
        given-names: "I."
    title: "PuLP: a linear programming toolkit for Python"
    year: 2011
    notes: >-
      The modelling layer used to emit the LP file. PuLP 4.x creates variables
      through LpProblem.add_variable and ships no bundled solver, which is why
      this package bounds pulp on both sides and solves through SciPy.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Breiman
        given-names: "L."
    title: "Random forests"
    journal: "Machine Learning"
    volume: 45
    issue: 1
    year: 2001
    notes: The estimator behind both learned components.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Wager
        given-names: "S."
      - family-names: Hastie
        given-names: "T."
      - family-names: Efron
        given-names: "B."
    title: >-
      Confidence intervals for random forests: the jackknife and the
      infinitesimal jackknife
    journal: "Journal of Machine Learning Research"
    volume: 15
    year: 2014
    notes: >-
      Why the per-tree spread reported by this package is a dispersion measure
      and not a confidence interval. Measured coverage of the plus-or-minus-two-
      sigma band is 0.947222 for the value model and 0.885000 for the
      compressibility model against a nominal 0.9545.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Pedregosa
        given-names: "F."
      - family-names: Varoquaux
        given-names: "G."
      - family-names: Gramfort
        given-names: "A."
    title: "Scikit-learn: machine learning in Python"
    journal: "Journal of Machine Learning Research"
    volume: 12
    year: 2011
    notes: The random-forest implementation used by both learned components.
  - type: standard
    title: "TM Synchronization and Channel Coding"
    institution:
      name: "Consultative Committee for Space Data Systems"
    number: "CCSDS 131.0-B-5"
    year: 2023
    notes: >-
      The existence of a discrete modulation-and-coding table, which is why a
      step profile is representable in downlinkpick.contacts and why the sine
      profile shipped with the generator is a convenience rather than a link
      budget. No numerical value in this repository is taken from it.
